1
00:00:01,170 --> 00:00:07,770
A laser only stays injection-locked while its frequency sits inside the master’s locking range.

2
00:00:07,850 --> 00:00:14,910
That frequency follows the laser’s temperature. Let the temperature wander, and the lock is gone.

3
00:00:14,990 --> 00:00:19,260
So the instrument has one job: hold the temperature still.

4
00:00:19,860 --> 00:00:22,710
How still? Zoom in.

5
00:00:22,940 --> 00:00:27,070
Past a kelvin. Past fifty millikelvin.

6
00:00:27,150 --> 00:00:34,890
One millikelvin, peak to peak, across a continuous one-hour hold — the whole window you see here.

7
00:00:34,970 --> 00:00:41,850
Everything in the design serves that number — under four constraints: cheap, easy to manufacture, modular, simple.

8
00:00:42,970 --> 00:00:46,020
The instrument is a closed loop.

9
00:00:46,290 --> 00:00:52,090
A thermistor senses the laser’s temperature; its resistance falls as it warms.

10
00:00:52,170 --> 00:00:57,060
A front end turns that resistance into a voltage ratio.

11
00:00:57,140 --> 00:01:02,120
An AD7124-8 converter turns the ratio into a 24-bit number.

12
00:01:02,200 --> 00:01:10,440
A Teensy 4.1 compares it with the setpoint and runs the PID locally — never over the network.

13
00:01:10,520 --> 00:01:17,590
Its PWM command drives a MAX1968, which pushes current through a thermoelectric cooler, either direction.

14
00:01:17,670 --> 00:01:20,970
Heat moves, the thermistor sees it, and the loop closes.

15
00:01:22,590 --> 00:01:25,730
Three different things get called stability.

16
00:01:26,010 --> 00:01:33,400
Resolution and short-term noise: can it see a tiny change, and how much do readings jump?

17
00:01:33,480 --> 00:01:39,210
Drift: the real temperature holds, but the reported number slowly moves.

18
00:01:39,290 --> 00:01:44,680
And absolute accuracy: how close the reading is to the truth.

19
00:01:44,760 --> 00:01:51,940
Accuracy we can live without — the working point is found on the bench. Drift is the one that matters.

20
00:01:53,770 --> 00:01:55,790
Here is why.

21
00:01:56,050 --> 00:02:05,860
The controller can only act on the number the electronics give it. It heats or cools until that number matches the setpoint.

22
00:02:05,940 --> 00:02:13,420
Now suppose the real temperature has not changed, but the electronics slowly begin to report high.

23
00:02:13,500 --> 00:02:20,960
The controller believes it, and cools the laser until the displayed value returns to the setpoint.

24
00:02:21,040 --> 00:02:26,900
The reading now looks perfect. The laser is colder than it was.

25
00:02:26,980 --> 00:02:35,690
That error happens before the reading reaches the controller, so the loop cannot see it. More gain cannot help.

26
00:02:35,770 --> 00:02:40,120
Which is why most of the design effort goes into stopping the reading from wandering.

27
00:02:42,510 --> 00:02:45,860
The main tool is ratiometric measurement.

28
00:02:46,130 --> 00:02:54,790
The converter does not report volts. It reports the signal as a fraction of a reference — a ruler.

29
00:02:54,870 --> 00:03:02,330
Arrange the circuit so a change moves the signal and the ruler equally, and it cancels.

30
00:03:02,410 --> 00:03:07,820
Move only one, and it looks exactly like a temperature change.

31
00:03:07,900 --> 00:03:12,900
For scale: at 25 °C, one millikelvin is 44 parts per million of the thermistor’s resistance.

32
00:03:15,150 --> 00:03:21,060
Rather than argue about the best arrangement, the comparison board carries four of them.

33
00:03:22,100 --> 00:03:32,420
Arm one drives the thermistor with the converter’s own current and rules it against a 22 kΩ resistor, so current drift cancels.

34
00:03:32,500 --> 00:03:40,500
But that ruler is also a ceiling: once the thermistor passes 22 kΩ — around 8 °C — the reading clips.

35
00:03:41,190 --> 00:03:54,090
Arm two is a divider from a precision 2.5 V reference, measured against the whole divider — so the source, its buffer and the track drop all cancel.

36
00:03:54,170 --> 00:04:01,140
Arms three and four are one divider with two power sources, chosen by a jumper.

37
00:04:01,220 --> 00:04:06,740
Three’s divider supply and its ruler are independent, so nothing cancels.

38
00:04:06,820 --> 00:04:14,190
Any drift between them lands straight on the reading — one kelvin of board change eats the entire budget. Three is out.

39
00:04:16,740 --> 00:04:26,500
Arm one has no clear edge in drift or in channel count, and its range is bounded — so it steps aside too.

40
00:04:26,990 --> 00:04:31,700
That leaves two and four, and they are genuinely tied.

41
00:04:32,060 --> 00:04:39,980
Arm two has the lowest costed drift — about 0.21 millikelvin for every kelvin the board moves.

42
00:04:40,060 --> 00:04:47,180
But its ruler needs a dedicated reference input pin, so it stops at two channels.

43
00:04:47,260 --> 00:04:56,520
Arm four gives up a little drift and needs no reference pins at all — so it scales to eight.

44
00:04:56,600 --> 00:05:00,380
Precision against expandability. The board is how we find out which we would rather have.

45
00:05:03,190 --> 00:05:06,070
Why build it at all?

46
00:05:06,370 --> 00:05:15,890
Commercial laser temperature controllers are expensive — easily over a thousand dollars — and their performance you simply have to trust.

47
00:05:15,970 --> 00:05:24,260
A laser’s frequency depends on current and temperature. Control those two, and the whole system gets dramatically simpler.

48
00:05:24,340 --> 00:05:31,070
And a temperature controller is versatile equipment — useful far beyond one lab.

49
00:05:31,150 --> 00:05:37,000
The design, the calculations and the board are all open. Come build it with us.

